<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01XS">Exercise <span data-tag="01XS">5.4.6.2</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a simplicial set and let $W$ be a collection of edges of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which has the two-out-of-six property. Show that $W$ has the two-out-of-three property. That is, for any $2$-simplex $\sigma $ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, if any two of the faces $d^{2}_0(\sigma )$, $d^{2}_1(\sigma )$, and $d^{2}_2(\sigma )$ belong to $W$, then so does the third. </p>
